Z||000000||Bigfieroman||12-20-2000||11:17 AM||||Is it possible to do this yourself in a machine shop?  Will it weaken the rotors too much?  Hole diameter?  Peformance enhancement?  please assist?<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||12-20-2000||12:40 PM||||bigfieroman,<P>Never cross drill any rotor in any application.  The ONLY reason it is still done in some forms of racing is to reduce unspring weight where every ounce counts.  It is only done anymore because it looks cool.  Anyone who says otherwise is just blowing smoke up... well you know where.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||13
Z||000002||Bigfieroman||12-20-2000||01:26 PM||||Are you sure?  I thought it could assist in brake cooling...What are the downsides of doing it that would make you feel so strongly about it?<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Will||12-20-2000||02:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by william_thorniii:<BR><B>bigfieroman,<P>Never cross drill any rotor in any application.  The ONLY reason it is still done in some forms of racing is to reduce unspring weight where every ounce counts.  It is only done anymore because it looks cool.  Anyone who says otherwise is just blowing smoke up... well you know where.  <P>William T. Thorn, III</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'll have to disagree with that one. When brakes are hot, the pads outgas. This gas forms a thin layer between the pad and rotor, reducing brake effectiveness. The cross drilling helps the gas escape from between the rotor and pad and recovers a bit of braking capacity.<BR>     Although I'm a big proponent of getting every last possible dyne of force out of a brake setup, I have to admit that cross drilling doesn't help much unless you visit the track regularly. It isn't as effective with street use and street pad compounds.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bigfieroman||12-20-2000||04:09 PM||||yeah...but it looks so cool!<P>------------------<BR>I once got a warning from a Police officer because he couldn't keep up with me around some twisties.  the prerequisite for a warning instead of a ticket was that I must let him drive it back and forth the way we had come.  Burned a quater tank of my gas!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Coconut||12-20-2000||04:30 PM||||What are you guys' thoughts on slotted rotors as opposed to cross drilled ones? Are they actually functional or eye candy as well? TIA<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Coconut's_Signature_1.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000006||Wannabe||12-20-2000||04:31 PM||||Yes, it looks cool and that's all. If you use your brakes hard enough that you need extra cooling, you will certainly crack cross drilled rotors.||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||12-20-2000||06:01 PM||||Cross drilling can weaken the rotor if it's not done properly. It does very little for cooling. It does help with waste management in racing, but does little during normal street driving. It's a waste of money and time on daily drivers.||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||12-20-2000||07:47 PM||||I knew I was going to get drawn into another argument...<P>Let me start off my statement with the quote from Grassroots Motorsports (February 2001).<P>"Crossdrilling your rotors might look neat, but what is ti really doing for you?  Well, unless your car is using brakes pads from the '40s or '50s, not a whole lot."<P>It is important to understand the function of brakes and how they stop the car.  Some common misconceptions about breaks are that larger pedal ratio/rotors/calipers/master cylendars/etc will stop the car quicker.  This is not in fact the case.  They might improve aspects of braking but all things considered they do not stop the car.  What stops the car?  The conversion of kinetic energy to heat and dissipating that heat is what stops a car.  <P>I will agree that by optimizing components between the brake pedal all the way back to the rotors that one will alter the pedal feel and clamp pressure.  Furthermore, bigger rotors do make sense if a car has a brake temperature problem.  Once you exceed the required thermal load of your braking system, it is just for looks.  <P>Back to cross-drilling... It is important to understand the history behind it first before we can sucessfully argue its benefits. <P>It was found that at extremely high temperatures often seen in off-road racing applications that organic pads would emit a gas.  This gas would in fact reduce braking effectiveness.  As the brakes continued to rise in temperature the gas could expand further and render the vehicles braking system virtually useless.  Someone came up with the ideas of drilling holes across the face to give the gas an escape route, not to reduce heat of the rotor.  <P>Also in racing applications when heated excessively, organic pads undergo a phenominon called "glazing".  Basically the material properties of the pad change at the surface of the pad that touches the rotor.  This new layer is of a significantly reduced coefficient of friction.  It was advantageous to rough up the pad continuously in race type situations to prevent glazing of the pads.  This significantly reduces pad life and would not be recomended for street use.  <P>New pad compounds have been developed that do NOT have the gassing out properties of earlier pads.  Also new pad compounds have eliminated the "glazing" phenomenon.  Why would one want to cause undue wear on their racing pads.  So from a material property standpoint, the need for cross drilling has been eliminated by new pad compounds.  <P>Lets assume that cross drilling was created as an attempt to reduce temperature.  Lets look at the theory that would be behind that concept.  In race applications, when braking, one is preparing to enter a corner or in other words the wheels are pointed straight.  Asuming that caster is set near neutral, the holes provided for airflow are perpindicular to the airflow.  It does not take a course in fluid dynamics to tell you that the majority of the flow will not be diverted to run through the holes in your rotors.  Therefore the thought would have been unfounded.  The flow through the holes would be such that it would not significantly reduce the heat of the rotor.  <P>Finally the reduction of heat that can be realized by cross drilling is often misrepresented.  Yes, it will remove more heat from the rotor.  It will not reduce the overall temperature significantly.  Why not?  Well the mass of a body has a great bearing on its temperature.  It is common knowledge among the racing community that the reduction of mass raises the rotor temperature more than the cross drilling reduced. <P>Please note the fact that I have not even addressed the disadvantages of having hundreds of stress risers in ones rotors.  If one does any machining/welding on their rotors I would hope that they properly relieve the stresses raised by their processes.  <P>There you have it.  That is my story and I am sticking to it.  <P>Slotted rotors are a different story, but my fingers are tired.  <P>William T. Thorn, III<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||12-20-2000||08:45 PM||||Yeah! What WT3 said!||||reg||1
Z||000010||sluppy123||12-21-2000||07:50 PM||||If they were my brakes I wouldn't want holes drilled in them(unless they were carbon fiber rotors), regardless if it helps braking or not. Cast Iron is brittle enough, why create more places for fracture's to form. <P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>85' SC & 87' GT-Leather, Lowered and On The Bottle<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=247832&a=1843773&p=29628235&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
